49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.150 Passage of internal inspection devices. > 49 CFR 192.150(b)(6)
(b) This section does not apply to: (1) Manifolds;
(6) Transmission lines, operated in conjunction with a distribution system which are installed in Class 4 locations;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.150 Passage of internal inspection devices. > 49 CFR 192.150(b)(7)
(b) This section does not apply to: (1) Manifolds;
(7) Offshore transmission lines, except transmission lines 10 3/4 inches (273 millimeters) or more in outside diameter on which construction begins after December 28, 2005, that run from platform to platform or platform to shore unless—

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.150 Passage of internal inspection devices. > 49 CFR 192.150(b)(7)(ii)
(b) This section does not apply to: (1) Manifolds;
(7) Offshore transmission lines, except transmission lines 10 3/4 inches (273 millimeters) or more in outside diameter on which construction begins after December 28, 2005, that run from platform to platform or platform to shore unless—
(ii) If the design includes taps for lateral connections, the operator can demonstrate, based on investigation or experience, that there is no reasonably practical alternative under the design circumstances to the use of a tap that will obstruct the passage of instrumented internal inspection devices;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.150 Passage of internal inspection devices. > 49 CFR 192.150(b)(8)
(b) This section does not apply to: (1) Manifolds;
(8) Gathering lines; and

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.150 Passage of internal inspection devices. > 49 CFR 192.150(b)(9)
(b) This section does not apply to: (1) Manifolds;
(9) Other piping that, under § 190.9 of this chapter, the Administrator finds in a particular case would be impracticable to design and construct to accommodate the passage of instrumented internal inspection devices.

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.161 Supports and anchors. > 49 CFR 192.161(a)(1)
(a) Each pipeline and its associated equipment must have enough anchors or supports to:
(1) Prevent undue strain on connected equipment;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.179 Transmission line valves. > 49 CFR 192.179(h)(1)
(h) The valve spacing requirements of paragraph (a) of this section do not apply to pipe replacements on a pipeline if the distance between each point on the pipeline and the nearest valve does not exceed:
(1) Four (4) miles in Class 4 locations, with a total spacing between valves no greater than 8 miles;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.187 Vaults: Sealing, venting, and ventilation. > 49 CFR 192.187(a)(1)
(a) When the internal volume exceeds 200 cubic feet (5.7 cubic meters):
(1) The vault or pit must be ventilated with two ducts, each having at least the ventilating effect of a pipe 4 inches (102 millimeters) in diameter;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.187 Vaults: Sealing, venting, and ventilation. > 49 CFR 192.187(b)(1)
(b) When the internal volume is more than 75 cubic feet (2.1 cubic meters) but less than 200 cubic feet (5.7 cubic meters):
(1) If the vault or pit is sealed, each opening must have a tight fitting cover without open holes through which an explosive mixture might be ignited, and there must be a means for testing the internal atmosphere before removing the cover;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.321 Installation of plastic pipe. > 49 CFR 192.321(h)(1)
(h) Plastic pipe may be installed on bridges provided that it is:
(1) Installed with protection from mechanical damage, such as installation in a metallic casing;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.327 Cover. > 49 CFR 192.327(d)(1)
(d) A main may be installed with less than 24 inches (610 millimeters) of cover if the law of the State or municipality:
(1) Establishes a minimum cover of less than 24 inches (610 millimeters);

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.355 Customer meters and regulators: Protection from damage. > 49 CFR 192.355(b)(1)
(b) Service regulator vents and relief vents. Service regulator vents and relief vents must terminate outdoors, and the outdoor terminal must—
(1) Be rain and insect resistant;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.361 Service lines: Installation. > 49 CFR 192.361(e)(1)
(e) Installation of service lines into buildings. Each underground service line installed below grade through the outer foundation wall of a building must:
(1) In the case of a metal service line, be protected against corrosion;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.361 Service lines: Installation. > 49 CFR 192.361(f)(1)
(f) Installation of service lines under buildings. Where an underground service line is installed under a building:
(1) It must be encased in a gas tight conduit;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.367 Service lines: General requirements for connections to main piping. > 49 CFR 192.367(b)(1)
(b) Compression-type connection to main. Each compression-type service line to main connection must:
(1) Be designed and installed to effectively sustain the longitudinal pull-out or thrust forces caused by contraction or expansion of the piping, or by anticipated external or internal loading;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.381 Service lines: Excess flow valve performance standards. > 49 CFR 192.381(a)(1)
(a) Excess flow valves (EFVs) to be used on service lines that operate continuously throughout the year at a pressure not less than 10 p.s.i. (69 kPa) gage must be manufactured and tested by the manufacturer according to an industry specification, or the manufacturer's written specification, to ensure that each valve will:
(1) Function properly up to the maximum operating pressure at which the valve is rated;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.381 Service lines: Excess flow valve performance standards. > 49 CFR 192.381(a)(2)
(a) Excess flow valves (EFVs) to be used on service lines that operate continuously throughout the year at a pressure not less than 10 p.s.i. (69 kPa) gage must be manufactured and tested by the manufacturer according to an industry specification, or the manufacturer's written specification, to ensure that each valve will:
(2) Function properly at all temperatures reasonably expected in the operating environment of the service line;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.383 Excess flow valve installation. > 49 CFR 192.383(b)(1)
(b) Installation required. An EFV installation must comply with the performance standards in § 192.381. After April 14, 2017, each operator must install an EFV on any new or replaced service line serving the following types of services before the line is activated:
(1) A single service line to one SFR;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.383 Excess flow valve installation. > 49 CFR 192.383(b)(2)
(b) Installation required. An EFV installation must comply with the performance standards in § 192.381. After April 14, 2017, each operator must install an EFV on any new or replaced service line serving the following types of services before the line is activated:
(2) A branched service line to a SFR installed concurrently with the primary SFR service line (i.e., a single EFV may be installed to protect both service lines);

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.383 Excess flow valve installation. > 49 CFR 192.383(b)(3)
(b) Installation required. An EFV installation must comply with the performance standards in § 192.381. After April 14, 2017, each operator must install an EFV on any new or replaced service line serving the following types of services before the line is activated:
(3) A branched service line to a SFR installed off a previously installed SFR service line that does not contain an EFV;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.383 Excess flow valve installation. > 49 CFR 192.383(c)(1)
(c) Exceptions to excess flow valve installation requirement. An operator need not install an excess flow valve if one or more of the following conditions are present:
(1) The service line does not operate at a pressure of 10 psig or greater throughout the year;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.383 Excess flow valve installation. > 49 CFR 192.383(c)(2)
(c) Exceptions to excess flow valve installation requirement. An operator need not install an excess flow valve if one or more of the following conditions are present:
(2) The operator has prior experience with contaminants in the gas stream that could interfere with the EFV's operation or cause loss of service to a customer;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.461 External corrosion control: Protective coating. > 49 CFR 192.461(a)(1)
(a) Each external protective coating, whether conductive or insulating, applied for the purpose of external corrosion control must—
(1) Be applied on a properly prepared surface;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.461 External corrosion control: Protective coating. > 49 CFR 192.461(a)(2)
(a) Each external protective coating, whether conductive or insulating, applied for the purpose of external corrosion control must—
(2) Have sufficient adhesion to the metal surface to effectively resist underfilm migration of moisture;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.461 External corrosion control: Protective coating. > 49 CFR 192.461(a)(3)
(a) Each external protective coating, whether conductive or insulating, applied for the purpose of external corrosion control must—
(3) Be sufficiently ductile to resist cracking;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.473 External corrosion control: Interference currents. > 49 CFR 192.473(c)(1)
(c) For onshore gas transmission pipelines, the program required by paragraph (a) of this section must include:
(1) Interference surveys for a pipeline system to detect the presence and level of any electrical stray current. Interference surveys must be conducted when potential monitoring indicates a significant increase in stray current, or when new potential stray current sources are introduced, such as through co-located pipelines, structures, or high voltage alternating current (HVAC) power lines, including from additional generation, a voltage up-rating, additional lines, new or enlarged power substations, or new pipelines or other structures;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.473 External corrosion control: Interference currents. > 49 CFR 192.473(c)(2)
(c) For onshore gas transmission pipelines, the program required by paragraph (a) of this section must include:
(2) Analysis of the results of the survey to determine the cause of the interference and whether the level could cause significant corrosion, impede safe operation, or adversely affect the environment or public;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.475 Internal corrosion control: General. > 49 CFR 192.475(b)(1)
(b) Whenever any pipe is removed from a pipeline for any reason, the internal surface must be inspected for evidence of corrosion. If internal corrosion is found—
(1) The adjacent pipe must be investigated to determine the extent of internal corrosion;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.476 Internal corrosion control: Design and construction of transmission line. > 49 CFR 192.476(a)(1)
(a) Design and construction. Except as provided in paragraph (b) of this section, each new transmission line and each replacement of line pipe, valve, fitting, or other line component in a transmission line must have features incorporated into its design and construction to reduce the risk of internal corrosion. At a minimum, unless it is impracticable or unnecessary to do so, each new transmission line or replacement of line pipe, valve, fitting, or other line component in a transmission line must:
(1) Be configured to reduce the risk that liquids will collect in the line;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.503 General requirements. > 49 CFR 192.503(b)(1)
(b) The test medium must be liquid, air, natural gas, or inert gas that is—
(1) Compatible with the material of which the pipeline is constructed;

49 CFR Part 192 — Transportation of Natural and Other Gas by Pipeline: Minimum Federal Safety Standards — § 192.503 General requirements. > 49 CFR 192.503(e)(1)
